ogles3.library 1.0 (release 1.0-beta1) for AmigaOS 3.x on PiStorm
==================================================================

OpenGL ES 3.1 for 68k AmigaOS, drawn by the Raspberry Pi 4's GPU (Broadcom
V3D 4.2) through PiStorm and Emu68. Programs get OpenGL ES 3.1 contexts,
and OpenGL ES 3.0 and 2.0 ones when they ask: compute shaders, storage
buffers and images, instancing, uniform buffers, transform feedback,
multisampled framebuffers, GLSL ES 3.10 and 3.00, and everything of
OpenGL ES 2.0. The agl* context calls are those of AmigaOS 4's
ogles2.library.

This is a beta. Every part of the Khronos conformance tests for OpenGL
ES 3.1 that applies passes on an A1200, group by group; the complete runs
of the OpenGL ES 2.0, 3.0 and 3.1 lists are still to come (see
"Conformance"). Please report what goes wrong.

ogles3 and ogles2
-----------------
ogles3.library is a new library beside ogles2.library 1.x (OpenGL ES 2.0),
not a replacement for it: programs written for ogles2 keep using
ogles2.library, and both can be installed at once. Each has its own
shader compiler (ogles3's is LIBS:v3dglslc3) and its own cache of compiled
shaders (OGLES3Cache:). Programs use ogles3 when they are built with its
SDK.

Requirements
------------
- An Amiga with a PiStorm and a Raspberry Pi 4B or CM4 (the GPU is the Pi's)
- Emu68 (tested: 1.1.0-beta.1)
- AmigaOS 3.x (tested: 3.2) with Picasso96 or CyberGraphX
- A 32-bit RTG screen for programs that render into a window

What is in this archive
-----------------------
  Install-ogles3      the installer (an AmigaDOS script, see below)
  Libs/ogles3.library the library
  Libs/v3dglslc3      the shader compiler (Mesa's V3D compiler, 19 MB)
  C/OGLES3Log         writes the library's log while a program runs
  Examples/           small programs with their compiled shaders
  SDK/                headers, import libraries, documentation and the
                      examples' sources, for programmers (only in
                      ogles3-1.0-beta1-sdk.lha; see "For programmers")
  LICENSE, licences/  licences and notices

Installing
----------
From a Shell, in this drawer:

    Execute Install-ogles3

It asks before each step:
- copies ogles3.library to LIBS: (an older LIBS:v3dglslc3 is replaced
  without asking, since the library uses that one first);
- copies the shader compiler v3dglslc3 to LIBS: (optional, see "Shaders");
- makes the drawer SYS:OGLES3Cache and adds "Assign OGLES3Cache:
  SYS:OGLES3Cache" to S:User-Startup, so compiled shaders are kept across
  reboots (optional; without it they are kept in T: until the next reboot);
- copies OGLES3Log to C: (optional).
"Execute Install-ogles3 ALL" does all four without asking. Nothing of an
installed ogles2.library is touched.

To remove it: delete LIBS:ogles3.library, LIBS:v3dglslc3, C:OGLES3Log and
SYS:OGLES3Cache, and the lines between ";BEGIN ogles3" and ";END ogles3"
in S:User-Startup.

Using it without installing
---------------------------
A program finds ogles3.library and the compiler in its own drawer when they
are not installed: the library in LIBS:, else PROGDIR:libs/, else beside the
program; the compiler likewise (LIBS:v3dglslc3, PROGDIR:libs/v3dglslc3,
PROGDIR:v3dglslc3). So a program can be shipped with a libs/ drawer holding
ogles3.library (and v3dglslc3, if it needs to compile shaders). A
program's own copy also replaces an installed one that is too old for it,
unless another program is using the old one at that moment (AmigaOS keeps
one ogles3.library in memory for all programs).

To try the examples this way: copy Libs/ogles3.library into Examples/libs/
and run the programs in Examples/.

Shaders
-------
The GPU runs machine code compiled from each program's GLSL. ogles3 finds
a program's compiled form ("binary", a .v3dp file named by a hash of the
GLSL text) in the program's shaders/ drawer, beside the program, or in
OGLES3Cache: (or T:). When there is none, the compiler v3dglslc3 compiles
it (about 0.2 s per program; its first start takes about a second) and the
result goes into OGLES3Cache:, so the next run needs no compiler. Some
draws need another variant of a program (for float or integer render
targets, for instance); the compiler makes it at that draw, and it is
kept in the cache too.

Programs that ship their binaries (as the examples do) run without the
compiler. Programs that make shaders at run time need it.

The examples
------------
  Examples/triangle   a turning coloured triangle in a window
  Examples/cube       a textured cube with a depth buffer, in a window you
                      can resize
  Examples/boingocean the Boing ball floating on a ray-marched ocean, all
                      drawn by shaders; drag with the mouse to move the
                      camera, keys 1 to 4 draw it at 1/1 to 1/4 size
  Examples/boing2026  the 1984 Boing Ball remade: it bounces in a neon room
                      with a soft shadow, a reflecting floor, bloom, 4x
                      antialiasing and its "boing" on Paula; "boing2026
                      TOUR" shows each effect in turn. Its keys and
                      options: Examples/boing2026.txt
  Examples/boing2026-ogles3
                      Boing 2026 in OpenGL ES 3: faster, with sparks where
                      the ball hits, dust in the light's beam and shadows
                      that harden where it touches; key E switches to the
                      ES 2.0 way to compare. Examples/boing2026-ogles3.txt
Close the window or press Esc to quit. Their .v3dp files are in
Examples/shaders/: they run without the compiler.

Logging
-------
The library logs to T:ogles3.log, or to the file named in the environment
variable OGLES3/LogFile (SetEnv OGLES3/LogFile RAM:ogles3.log). OGLES3Log
writes the buffered log while a program still runs. A program that fails
usually says why there.

For programmers
---------------
The SDK comes in two forms: ogles3-1.0-beta1-sdk.lha (this archive with
SDK/ added) and ogles3-1.0-beta1-sdk.zip (the SDK alone, for building on a
PC with a cross compiler). It holds:

SDK/include    <proto/ogles3.h> (every OpenGL ES 3.1 function and agl*),
               <libraries/ogles3.h> (tags, errors, extra enumerants), the
               dispatch table, and Khronos' GLES2/, GLES3/ and KHR/ headers
SDK/lib        libogles3.a (GCC) and ogles3.lib (vbcc), the import library
SDK/doc        GettingStarted.txt: writing, building, debugging and shipping
               a program, moving one from ogles2.library, and a prompt for
               working with an AI assistant; ogles3.doc: the agl* calls,
               context versions, shaders and binaries, extensions and
               deviations
SDK/examples   the examples' sources and a Makefile (GCC and vbcc)

A context is OpenGL ES 3.1 unless the program asks for another with the
tag OGLES3_CCT_PISTORM_ES_VERSION (20 gives OpenGL ES 2.0). Source written
for ogles2.library 1.x builds with its #include lines changed to
<proto/ogles3.h>: the OGLES2_ names are still defined.

The import library and the headers are MIT-licensed: programs of any
licence may use them. The source of everything here, the compiler's
included, is at https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles3.

Conformance
-----------
The Khronos dEQP-GLES31 tests, run group by group on an A1200: every case
that applies passes; those of OpenGL ES 3.2 and of extensions the library
does not offer report "not supported". dEQP-GLES3 passed group by group
while OpenGL ES 3.0 was built, and the whole dEQP-GLES2 list passed in
ogles2.library 1.3. The complete runs of the three lists, and the speed
comparison with ogles2 1.3, come before the final 1.0. The SDK's
ogles3.doc lists the deviations: cube maps always filter seamlessly,
multisampling is always 4x, 32-bit float textures filter nearest, and
there are no geometry or tessellation shaders (OpenGL ES 3.2).

Licence
-------
ogles3.library incorporates PiStorm3D's V3D backend and is distributed
under the Hyperion MiniGL Open Source License (licences/
License_MiniGL_Library.txt): for AmigaOS only, with the source published.
The import library, headers, examples and OGLES3Log are MIT. v3dglslc3 is
Mesa (MIT; licences/Mesa/). LICENSE lists every part.

